# Trivy Vulnerability Ignore Policy
# This file documents known vulnerabilities that are either:
# 1. Not exploitable in container context
# 2. Fixed upstream but awaiting propagation to shipped binaries/packages
# 3. Have acceptable risk for devcontainer usage
#
# Each entry requires an Expiration date. Expired entries cause CI to fail,
# forcing periodic review. Tracking: https://github.com/vig-os/devkit/issues/512
#
# EXPIRY GRID (#1337): every `Expiration:` date lands on a WEDNESDAY so the
# entry turns red on a Thursday, after the week's remediation data has landed
# and with two working days before the weekend. Rationale and the snapping rule
# (nearest Wednesday, shift <= 3 days) live in docs/CONTAINER_SECURITY.md. The
# dates below were snapped onto that grid on 2026-08-04, each extended by 1 day;
# no risk assessment was re-opened or changed by the snap.
#
# STAGGER (#1553), 2026-08-20: the three blocks below had all landed on
# 2026-09-02, sharing it with 24 .vulnixignore blocks and the
# dependency-review allow-list — 29 of 33 live entries on one day. They are
# three independent exceptions with three different remediation levers, so each
# now carries its own Wednesday, placed after the .vulnixignore passes so the
# two registers do not compete for the same review week: gh/Go stdlib
# 2026-10-21, jq 2026-10-28, jwt-token 2026-11-04. Scheduling only — no risk
# assessment below was re-opened or changed.

# ============================================================================
# RETIRED 2026-09-07 — the register is empty by remediation, not by deletion.
#
# The three blocks this register carried were all written against the Debian
# `:latest` image, whose Trivy job was removed with the Debian path (#642). The
# Nix image inherited the file but none of the findings: scanned with NO ignore
# file (trivy 0.72.0, `image --input <devkitImage tar> --scanners vuln,secret
# --severity HIGH,CRITICAL,MEDIUM`, closure built from the c25784012c pin), it
# reports 63 unique HIGH/CRITICAL/MEDIUM vulnerabilities and ZERO secret
# findings, and none of the three excepted IDs is among them. Each entry is
# tombstoned rather than renewed:
#
# - CVE-2026-42504 (#512, #564) — Go stdlib MIME header DoS, matched via the
#   `gh` binary Debian shipped on Go 1.26.3. Every gobinary in the Nix image
#   reports stdlib v1.26.7, past the 1.26.4 rebuild the block was waiting for,
#   so the finding cannot match. The lever the note said no weekly bump could
#   move has moved on its own, in nixpkgs.
# - jwt-token (#512) — Trivy secret-scan false positive on a JWT-like string in
#   typos crate test fixtures under `/opt/pre-commit-cache`. The Nix image has
#   no such path (prek caches at `/opt/prek-cache`) and ships typos as a
#   prebuilt binary, not its source fixtures; the secret scanner runs over the
#   image and finds nothing at all.
# - CVE-2026-32316, CVE-2026-40164 (#805, #512) — jq DoS/RCE scoped to Debian
#   `jq`/`libjq1` 1.6-2.1+deb12u1. The Nix image has no dpkg DB for Trivy's OS
#   scanner to match against and ships jq 1.8.2; the block's own escape
#   condition was "time-box the ignore until the Nix image ships", and it has.
#
# The file stays as the seam for genuine Nix-image Trivy findings and secret
# false positives (docs/CONTAINER_SECURITY.md). Adding an entry re-arms
# `check-expirations`; until then there is nothing to expire and nothing to
# review, which is the honest state of the register.
# ============================================================================
